.omo/evidence/ai-tutor-pdf-actions-katex-release/final/f1-plan-compliance.md:41:   F2 found that `classifyTutorLatex()` rejects ordinary single-variable expressions because `isPlainProse()` requires a command, digit, operator, bracket, or similar math symbol. As a result, `$x$`, `$a$`, and `$y$` remain dollar-delimited literal text. This violates the plan’s success criterion that AI Tutor formulas render cleanly and the user’s requirement that math not appear broken/plain.
.omo/evidence/ai-tutor-pdf-actions-katex-release/final/f2-code-quality.md:26:   `classifyTutorLatex()` calls `isPlainProse()`, and `isPlainProse()` returns literal when an expression has no command, digit, operator, bracket, or other symbol. That rejects normal high-school notation consisting of a single variable. AI tutor answers commonly say things like `$x$`, `$a$`, `$y$`, or "먼저 $y$를 정리해요"; those will remain visible as dollar-delimited text instead of KaTeX. This directly conflicts with the user's stated requirement that formulas not appear broken/plain in tutor answers.
